{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read source-text://queryformer-paper-v1 --start-line 128 --lines 68'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"queryformer-paper-v1\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.16548v1\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2609.16548v1\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"f8e114e22e2e8016610748784b98f2ffb59e5611f92ef03689e11f7aed4de3a6\",\n  \"total_lines\": 591,\n  \"start_line\": 128,\n  \"next_line\": 196,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"128: 4.2. Query-Driven Architecture\\n129: Design Principles.\\n130: (1)\\n131: Generate through attention, not projection alone. Cross-attention over NS tokens yields data-dependent query probes beyond projection-only summaries.\\n132: (2)\\n133: Refine through interaction. Queries are enriched via self-attention and cross-attention before they retrieve behavioral evidence.\\n134: (3)\\n135: Scale through diversity. The embedding matrix provides HH independent tokenization views, improving the explored scaling frontier.\\n136: 4.2.1. Query-Generation Pipeline\\n137: The query-generation pipeline updates the NS context and then constructs final query probes:\\n138: (2)\\n139: N′\\\\displaystyle N^{\\\\prime}\\n140: =FeatureInteract⁡(Nu,Ni),\\\\displaystyle=\\\\mathrm{FeatureInteract}(N_{u},N_{i}),\\n141: (3)\\n142: R\\\\displaystyle R\\n143: =SeqRetrieve⁡(Ni′,B),\\\\displaystyle=\\\\mathrm{SeqRetrieve}(N^{\\\\prime}_{i},B),\\n144: (4)\\n145: Q\\\\displaystyle Q\\n146: =QueryGenerate⁡(pool⁡(B),N′∪R),\\\\displaystyle=\\\\mathrm{QueryGenerate}(\\\\mathrm{pool}(B),N^{\\\\prime}\\\\cup R),\\n147: (5)\\n148: Y\\\\displaystyle Y\\n149: =Mix⁡(Q,N′,B).\\\\displaystyle=\\\\mathrm{Mix}(Q,N^{\\\\prime},B).\\n150: Here Ni′N^{\\\\prime}_{i} denotes the item-token slice of N′N^{\\\\prime}. This pipeline exposes the reusable bridge component of QueryFormer: NS tokens first retrieve sequence evidence, then generated query tokens re-query the refined NS context. The bridge targets the field–sequence interaction point of token-based recommenders: it takes field tokens and behavior tokens as input, and returns generated query probes plus mixed representations for downstream towers. Because its interface is token-in/token-out, the bridge is architecturally stackable with existing unified recommenders rather than tied to a single backbone. It is also computationally stackable: sequence queries are concatenated and evaluated by shared-parameter cross-attention, amortizing key/value projections and graphics processing unit (GPU) kernel launches instead of invoking cross-attention serially. Table 2 gives the operator-to-mechanism mapping.\\n151: Table 2 lists the four attention mechanisms organized into three architectural stages. Together they form a query-centric refinement pipeline: queries are enriched via self-attention, cross-interact with counterpart tokens, attend to behavioral sequences, and drive query generation through explicit token-to-query attention.\\n152: Table 2. Attention mechanisms by architectural stage. For SeqQueryCrossAttn, QBQ_{B} denotes behavior-initialized query probes.\\n153: Stage\\n154: Mechanism\\n155: Type\\n156: Interaction\\n157: Co-Transformer\\n158: QuerySelfAttn\\n159: SelfAttn\\n160: ⟨Nu,Nu⟩\\\\langle N_{u},N_{u}\\\\rangle, ⟨Ni,Ni⟩\\\\langle N_{i},N_{i}\\\\rangle\\n161: QueryCrossAttn\\n162: CrossAttn\\n163: ⟨Nu,Ni⟩\\\\langle N_{u},N_{i}\\\\rangle\\n164: QuerySeqCrossAttn\\n165: —\\n166: CrossAttn\\n167: ⟨Ni,B⟩\\\\langle N_{i},B\\\\rangle\\n168: SeqQueryCrossAttn\\n169: —\\n170: CrossAttn\\n171: ⟨QB,N⟩\\\\langle Q_{B},N\\\\rangle\\n172: 4.2.2. Embedding Matrix\\n173: Instead of a flat token sequence, QueryFormer uses an embedding matrix with HH independent columns. Each column tokenizes the same input through its own pipeline, producing a tensor of shape (Bs,H,T,dmodel)(B_{s},H,T,d_{\\\\text{model}}), where BsB_{s} is batch size and TT is token count. This implements view diversity in one batch-parallel forward pass and exposes a direct scaling axis. Figure 3 shows that increasing HH from 1 to 8 consistently improves validation AUC and LogLoss at fixed dmodel=272d_{\\\\text{model}}=272.\\n174: Training curves showing that wider embedding matrices achieve better final AUC and LogLoss.\\n175: Figure 3. Validation AUC and LogLoss vs. training steps for H∈{1,2,4,8}H\\\\in\\\\{1,2,4,8\\\\} at dmodel=272d_{\\\\text{model}}{=}272.Training curves showing that wider embedding matrices achieve better final AUC and LogLoss.\\n176: 4.2.3. Co-Transformer\\n177: The Co-Transformer contains two layers. Each layer first applies QuerySelfAttn within user and item token sets, then QueryCrossAttn between user and item tokens with gated fusion. This separates within-field refinement from cross-side interaction and lets us ablate the two effects independently.\\n178: 4.2.4. Retrieval\\n179: QuerySeqCrossAttn retrieves behavior evidence by using aggregated item NS tokens as queries over each of the four behavior domains. The retrieved vectors summarize item-relevant historical evidence and are appended to the NS token set.\\n180: 4.2.5. SeqQueryCrossAttn\\n181: SeqQueryCrossAttn generates Nq=2N_{q}=2 query probes per sequence domain. Mean/max-pooled behavior summaries initialize probes, and cross-attention over refined NS tokens makes them context-aware:\\n182: (6)\\n183: 𝐪d,k=CrossAttnd​(𝐪d,k(0),𝐍,𝐍).\\\\mathbf{q}_{d,k}=\\\\mathrm{CrossAttn}_{d}(\\\\mathbf{q}_{d,k}^{(0)},\\\\mathbf{N},\\\\mathbf{N}).\\n184: 4.3. Supporting Components\\n185: 4.3.1. NS Token Construction\\n186: NS tokens are built from user/item integer features, dense feature slices, temporal features, item ID, and four retrieved sequence tokens, yielding 26 NS tokens.\\n187: 4.3.2. Dense Feature Fusion\\n188: User and item dense features are processed by DenseFusionModel, which combines a low-rank DCN-V2 layer (L=1L=1, rank r=8r=8), a SiLU-activated MLP, and Squeeze-and-Excitation (SENet) recalibration (Hu et al., 2018).\\n189: 4.3.3. Sequence Token Embedding\\n190: Each behavior domain embeds side-information independently, projects it to dmodeld_{\\\\text{model}}, and adds a 65-bucket recency embedding.\\n191: 4.3.4. Blocks\\n192: Following HyFormer and OneTrans (Huang et al., 2026; Zhang et al., 2026), K=2K=2 lightweight processing blocks evolve behavior sequences with Swish-Gated Linear Unit (SwiGLU) encoders, attend from queries to sequences, and mix tokens through parameter-free permutation.\\n193: 4.4. Training Recipe\\n194: 4.4.1. Multi-Loss Supervision\\n195: The training objective combines binary cross-entropy (BCE) with an auxiliary mean absolute error (MAE) loss on log-transformed conversion time (λ=0.1\\\\lambda=0.1). Removing it reduces validation AUC from 0.84606 to 0.84590, a smaller effect than any attention ablation.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read source-text://unidot-paper-v1 --start-line 163 --lines 50'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unidot-paper-v1\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.16797v1\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2608.16797v1\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"7714c79aba968b84d631bb677ca87a16614add7785fff830fd99b93e33b39727\",\n  \"total_lines\": 1342,\n  \"start_line\": 163,\n  \"next_line\": 213,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"163: implicit—an emergent property of a deep interaction stack or\\n164: transformer. UniDot keeps it explicit wherever signal crosses the user–item or\\n165: candidate–history boundary, using the deeper machinery (token mixing, gated\\n166: MLPs, attention) to refine the operands of those dot-products rather than\\n167: replace the operation.\\n168: user / itemprofile fids+ pre-trained embsbehavioraldomains S1..S4S_{1}..S_{4}tokenizers (§4.2)𝐔,𝐈,𝐈h\\\\mathbf{U},\\\\mathbf{I},\\\\mathbf{I}_{h}seq pipeline (§4.3)embed once, share𝐇(1)..𝐇(S)\\\\mathbf{H}^{(1)}..\\\\mathbf{H}^{(S)}token-mixing busWukong / TokenMixer ×W\\\\times WMultiChannelSeqPoolMLP-Mixer fusion(FuseFFN)sequence-retrieval bus𝐈h\\\\mathbf{I}_{h} cross-attends each 𝐇(s)\\\\mathbf{H}^{(s)} + fusion FFN\\n169: macro-block ×L\\\\times Lclassifierρ\\\\rho + MLP →y^\\\\to\\\\hat{y}+ aux delay head[𝐔;𝐈][\\\\mathbf{U};\\\\mathbf{I}]𝐈h\\\\mathbf{I}_{h}viewspooledZmixLZ^{L}_{\\\\text{mix}}ZseqLZ^{L}_{\\\\text{seq}}FM Highway: [ϕ1;…;ϕL][\\\\phi^{1};\\\\dots;\\\\phi^{L}] (dots + Gram + cross-dots), bypasses fusionemb-skip signal eskipe_{\\\\text{skip}}\\n170: Figure 1. UniDot architecture (§4): two buses co-evolve through\\n171: LL macro-blocks, exchange state via the per-layer MLP-Mixer fusion, and the FM\\n172: Highway routes explicit per-layer dot-products past the fuser to the classifier.\\n173: UniDot stacks one homogeneous block LL times: two buses—a token-mixing\\n174: bus over user/item profile tokens and a sequence-retrieval bus whose\\n175: item tokens cross-attend the behavioral histories—exchange state each layer\\n176: through an MLP-Mixer fuser, while an FM Highway routes explicit\\n177: dot-product interactions straight to the classifier. Everything else\\n178: (§4) is how these pieces are made cheap, candidate-aware,\\n179: and scalable. Around this design—plus the training recipe behind our final\\n180: entry—we contribute four ideas.\\n181: (1) A dual-path, per-layer-parallel block: rather than cascading a\\n182: sequence module into an interaction backbone, each block runs two paths\\n183: concurrently—a non-sequential token-mixing path (a swappable\\n184: mixer slot: Wukong’s parallel LCB + FMB by default, a TokenMixer-style block as a\\n185: drop-in) and a sequence-retrieval path (item tokens cross-attend the\\n186: behavioral sequences)—which exchange state every layer via a canonical\\n187: MLP-Mixer fusion (§4.4), so profile and sequence signal co-evolve;\\n188: this interleaving is inspired by InterFormer (Zeng et al., 2025) and\\n189: Kunlun (Hou et al., 2026), from which UniDot differs in keeping the cross-boundary\\n190: signal an explicit dot-product.\\n191: (2) A shared, candidate-aware, multi-domain sequence pipeline: all\\n192: behavioral domains are embedded once and reshaped to a uniform per-position\\n193: width by a position-local fid-axis compression (§4.2); a DIN-style\\n194: conditionally gated SwiGLU injects candidate context into only the\\n195: gate, and a timestamp-interleaved merged stream models cross-domain\\n196: temporal patterns (§4.3).\\n197: (3) The FM Highway for explicit feature interaction: per-domain\\n198: query–key dot products, an aggregated Gram matrix, and cross-bus user–item dots\\n199: are concatenated across layers and fed directly to the classifier\\n200: (§4.4), bypassing the residual fusion path and preserving the\\n201: FM-style second-order signal a deep residual stack tends to wash out, at\\n202: negligible cost.\\n203: (4) Multi-path mutual learning over shared embeddings: we train\\n204: two UniDot paths jointly on one shared (and dominant) embedding table,\\n205: each regularized toward the other’s predictions (§4.6), pulling even a\\n206: single served path to a better minimum—a 1×\\\\times-cost model that would\\n207: itself place as runner-up (Table 1)—while the two-path mean\\n208: recovers the small remainder.\\n209: UniDot\\n210: finished as the runner-up on the Industrial track of the TAAC ×\\\\times KDD Cup\\n211: 2026 challenge under the official AUC metric,\\n212: reaching a final-leaderboard AUC of 0.83217 (Table 1). This result came from\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"The incumbent adds action and recency embeddings to each field key before scoring relevance. That leaves a testable uncertainty: do those additions help retrieval, or interfere with category and brand matching?\n\nI’ll prepare a four-way comparison of semantic-only versus mixed keys, crossed with target versus history-only queries. All variants will retain the same event values, parameter count, output width, and history-norm control. This tests the role of metadata in scoring; any performance benefit remains unmeasured."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nsource=Path('/workspace/candidate.py').read_text()\nheader='''\\\"\\\"\\\"Semantic retrieval keys versus metadata-mixed keys: a query-by-key factorial.\n\nInherited: DIN-style relation scorer, independent field softmaxes, aligned values,\nand target-independent pooled-norm matching from g2-r0001-target_separate.\nIntervention: omit action/recency from relevance keys only. Both metadata\nembeddings remain in every event value and in the reference norm. This is an\nablation hypothesis, not a new-attention priority claim. No optimizer/loss change.\n\\\"\\\"\\\"'''\nsource=header+source[source.index('\\\\nimport torch'):]\nsource=source.replace(\\\"'target_query': True, 'separate_reads': True,\\\", \\\"'target_query': True, 'separate_reads': True, 'semantic_keys': True,\\\")\nsource=source.replace(\\\"        self.separate_reads = self.config['separate_reads']\\\", \\\"        self.separate_reads = self.config['separate_reads']\\\\n        self.semantic_keys = self.config['semantic_keys']\\\")\nold='''    def _read(self, category, brand, common, mask, query):\n        category_score = self._score(query[:, 0], category + common)\n        brand_score = self._score(query[:, 1], brand + common)'''\nnew='''    def _read(self, category, brand, common, mask, query, semantic_keys=None):\n        use_semantic = self.semantic_keys if semantic_keys is None else semantic_keys\n        # The values below are unchanged: metadata is excluded from keys only.\n        category_key = category if use_semantic else category + common\n        brand_key = brand if use_semantic else brand + common\n        category_score = self._score(query[:, 0], category_key)\n        brand_score = self._score(query[:, 1], brand_key)'''\nassert old in source\nsource=source.replace(old,new)\n# Replace only diagnostics; the forward path otherwise retains the parent code.\nsource=source[:source.index('    def diagnostics(self, batch):')]+'''    def diagnostics(self, batch):\n        fields, category, brand, common, mask, pooled, target, context = self._encode(batch)\n        query = target if self.target_query else context\n        separate, joint, wc, wb, wj = self._read(category, brand, common, mask, query)\n        _, _, cc, cb, _ = self._read(category, brand, common, mask, context)\n        semantic, _, sc, sb, _ = self._read(category, brand, common, mask, query, True)\n        mixed, _, mc, mb, _ = self._read(category, brand, common, mask, query, False)\n        _, _, zc, zb, _ = self._read(category, brand, torch.zeros_like(common), mask, query)\n        selected_raw = separate if self.separate_reads else joint\n        selected = self._match_norm(selected_raw, pooled)\n        semantic_matched = self._match_norm(semantic, pooled)\n        mixed_matched = self._match_norm(mixed, pooled)\n        active = mask.any(-1)\n        active_float = active.float()\n        active_count = active_float.sum().clamp_min(1)\n        def mean_active(value):\n            return float((value * active_float).sum() / active_count)\n        weights = 0.5 * (wc + wb)\n        ref_norm = pooled.norm(dim=-1)\n        norm_error = (selected.norm(dim=-1) - ref_norm).abs()\n        cosine = ((semantic_matched * mixed_matched).sum(-1)\n                  / (semantic_matched.norm(dim=-1) * mixed_matched.norm(dim=-1)).clamp_min(1e-9))\n        entropy = -0.5 * ((wc * wc.clamp_min(1e-9).log()).sum(-1)\n                          + (wb * wb.clamp_min(1e-9).log()).sum(-1))\n        category_match = (mask & (batch['history_category'] > 0)\n                          & (batch['static'][:, self.category, None] > 0)\n                          & (batch['history_category'] == batch['static'][:, self.category, None]))\n        brand_match = (mask & (batch['history_brand'] > 0)\n                       & (batch['static'][:, self.brand, None] > 0)\n                       & (batch['history_brand'] == batch['static'][:, self.brand, None]))\n        decomposed = ((wc.unsqueeze(-1) * (category + 0.5 * common)).sum(1)\n                      + (wb.unsqueeze(-1) * (brand + 0.5 * common)).sum(1))\n        diagnostics = {\n            'nonempty_fraction': float(active_float.mean()),\n            'supplied_event_count_mean': float(mask.float().sum(-1).mean()),\n            'reference_norm_mean': float(ref_norm.mean()),\n            'selected_norm_mean': float(selected.norm(dim=-1).mean()),\n            'norm_absolute_error_max': float(norm_error.max()),\n            'norm_relative_error_max': float((norm_error / ref_norm.clamp_min(1e-6)).max()),\n            'norm_fallback_fraction': float((selected_raw.norm(dim=-1) <= 1e-6).float().mean()),\n            'empty_history_summary_norm_max': float((selected.norm(dim=-1) * (~active).float()).max()),\n            'padding_attention_mass_max': float((weights * (~mask).float()).sum(-1).max()),\n            'attention_sum_error_max': float((weights.sum(-1) - active_float).abs().max()),\n            'value_decomposition_error_max': float((separate - decomposed).abs().max()),\n            'semantic_mixed_summary_cosine_nonempty': mean_active(cosine),\n            'semantic_mixed_attention_l1_nonempty': mean_active(0.5 * ((sc - mc).abs().sum(-1)\n                                                                                    + (sb - mb).abs().sum(-1))),\n            'metadata_zeroed_attention_l1_nonempty': mean_active(0.5 * ((wc - zc).abs().sum(-1)\n                                                                                   + (wb - zb).abs().sum(-1))),\n            'field_attention_l1_nonempty': mean_active((wc - wb).abs().sum(-1)),\n            'attention_l1_vs_context_nonempty': mean_active(0.5 * ((wc - cc).abs().sum(-1)\n                                                                               + (wb - cb).abs().sum(-1))),\n            'field_attention_entropy_nonempty': mean_active(entropy),\n            'joint_attention_entropy_nonempty': mean_active(-(wj * wj.clamp_min(1e-9).log()).sum(-1)),\n            'category_bucket_match_mass_nonempty': mean_active((wc * category_match.float()).sum(-1)),\n            'brand_bucket_match_mass_nonempty': mean_active((wb * brand_match.float()).sum(-1)),\n            'selected_mean_log2_age_nonempty': mean_active((weights * torch.log2(\n                batch['history_age_seconds'].clamp_min(0) + 1)).sum(-1)),\n            'uniform_mean_log2_age_nonempty': mean_active((mask.float() * torch.log2(\n                batch['history_age_seconds'].clamp_min(0) + 1)).sum(-1)\n                / mask.sum(-1).clamp_min(1)),\n            'common_to_semantic_event_norm_ratio': float((common.norm(dim=-1) * mask.float()).sum()\n                / ((0.5 * (category.norm(dim=-1) + brand.norm(dim=-1)) * mask.float()).sum().clamp_min(1e-6))),\n            'uses_target_query': float(self.target_query),\n            'uses_semantic_keys': float(self.semantic_keys),\n            'uses_separate_reads': float(self.separate_reads),\n        }\n        for action_id in (1, 2, 3, 4):\n            action_mask = (mask & (batch['history_type'] == action_id)).float()\n            diagnostics['action_%d_attention_mass_nonempty' % action_id] = mean_active(\n                (weights * action_mask).sum(-1))\n            diagnostics['action_%d_uniform_share_nonempty' % action_id] = mean_active(\n                action_mask.sum(-1) / mask.sum(-1).clamp_min(1))\n        return diagnostics\n'''\nvariants=[('semantic_target', True, True),('mixed_target', True, False),\n          ('semantic_context', False, True),('mixed_context', False, False)]\nfor name,target,semantic in variants:\n text=source.replace(\\\"'target_query': True, 'separate_reads': True, 'semantic_keys': True,\\\",\n                     \\\"'target_query': %s, 'separate_reads': True, 'semantic_keys': %s,\\\" % (target,semantic))\n Path('/workspace/'+name+'.py').write_text(text)\nprint('Created four standalone model modules; incumbent candidate.py preserved.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
